module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_serving_tier_sized_as_a_cache_while_the_store_policy_makes_it_the_only_path_to_disk

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

// ONE TIER, TWO JOBS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS SIZED FOR. A KV hierarchy with a RAM tier in front of a disk
// tier is usually a cache: the front tier holds what is hot, the back tier holds everything, and the size of the
// front tier is an efficiency question — too small costs hits, never correctness. That reading is right for half
// the store policies that can be put on such a hierarchy and WRONG for the one srv1's replicas run. Under a
// policy that writes a checkpoint to the disk tier only as it leaves the RAM tier, the front tier is not a cache
// with an optional backing store; it is the STAGING BUFFER on the only path a checkpoint has to disk. Size it as
// a cache and it fills; and filling is not a slowdown, it is the path on which a checkpoint is deleted before it
// was ever written — the eviction that was supposed to persist it is the operation that destroys it, with no
// retry, no watermark and no protected share able to recover bytes that never existed anywhere else.
//
// THE CLASS IS NOT "a cache too small". It is: a tier whose SIZE was derived from a hit-rate model is loaded
// under a policy that gives it a durability obligation, and no row between the two states the obligation. The
// sibling row gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_capacity_default_sized_for_one_workload_shape_inherited_by_another
// is the same drift in the OTHER direction — a figure correct when written, inherited unchanged by a workload
// that grew. This row is about a figure that was never about this quantity at all: the number answered
// "how much do we want hot?", and the policy asked "can you hold every in-flight checkpoint at once?". The two
// questions have no common unit, which is why the size could stay unchanged for sixteen days across four
// restarts and three policy discussions without anything being able to say it was wrong.

data a_serving_tier_sized_as_a_cache_while_the_store_policy_makes_it_the_only_path_to_disk: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_serving_tier_sized_as_a_cache_while_the_store_policy_makes_it_the_only_path_to_disk" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: MechanicallyPreventable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the launcher and the engine-args render DERIVE the RAM tier from the two facts that bound it — the per-replica concurrent population resolved on the replica's profile row and the per-conversation cost row priced on that engine's own KV pool density — instead of reading a declared size field, and the store policy that decides whether the tier carries the durability obligation at all is read by the build that already digests the admitted runtime image (the file whose replacement digest gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_launch carries) rather than transcribed from an operator read; at that point a tier below the floor has no row to be written in and no policy claim to be misremembered, which is where this class stops being refused and becomes unconstructible"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a serving replica's RAM KV tier is a size the model derived from a hit-rate wish, the engine is configured so that the disk tier is reachable only through that RAM tier, and the population that can hold the tier busy at once is nowhere related to its size. Nothing refuses, because no shape became invalid: the tier is a number, the policy is a string, and the two never meet in a row. The host keeps answering — more slowly, with a warm cache that never stays warm — and the only artifact is a miss rate the reader attributes to the workload.",

    "**FOUND, AND NOT DISCHARGED BY THE RAISE: 2026-11-24, srv1, at L1 = 12 GiB / L2 = 125 GiB (the figures this repository carried from 2026-11-08, when the tier was chosen as a small RAM cache in front of a big disk: srv1_serving_gpu_launcher's own comment, 'Disk-first KV (2026-11-08, operator): small 12 GiB RAM tier L1'). WHAT WAS SEEN, with the instrument named and the standing stated: the operator's session read LMCache's own lines in ~/lil/serve/serve.log and ~/lil/serve-gpu1/serve.log on srv1 — the tier at 90-99% full; the evict-immediately path deleting fresh pages 0.07-1 s after creation without writing them (log suppressed_total ~740k pages on GPU0 over 3.9 h); attempted saves granted 48% / 43% of the bytes asked for on GPU0 / GPU1 (2,760 / 4,224 saves); a conversation returning to the same card after 5-60 min missing 50-75% of the time. After the tier was raised to 48 GiB (replicas restarted 19:28:12 and 19:38:04 UTC), same-card unchanged-prefix warmth read 96-99% against 86-90% before, measured on vLLM's own counters vllm:prefix_cache_\{hits,queries}_total and vllm:external_prefix_cache_\{hits,queries}_total, sampled twice and differenced. THEN, AT THE SAME TIER, THE LOSS CONTINUED: at 06:00 UTC on 2026-11-25 the delete-fresh-checkpoints-before-store path was still running — 187 events on GPU1 and 56 on GPU0 per 15 minutes. THE RAISE MOVED THE PROBLEM; IT DID NOT DISCHARGE THE CONTRACT. Every figure in this receipt is relayed from the operator's session, not re-taken by this repository, and is carried at that standing (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility — an inference stays a bet, a receipt stays a receipt). One standing caveat travels with the GPU1 numbers after 04:49 UTC: that replica was stopped 01:31-04:49 and its LMCache disk tier was deleted, so anything read from it afterwards is read against a REFILLING tier, which makes a warmth figure read there optimistic rather than representative. The wall this row demands is NOT derived from these numbers; it is derived from the store policy, the per-conversation cost row and the in-flight population, so it stands without them.",

    "**THE COPIES WERE NOT ALL IN THE LAUNCH PATH, WHICH IS WHY THE SIZE COULD STAY WRONG QUIETLY: the same tier had SIX values in this repository, and only two of them were ever rendered. srv1_serving_replica_layout's launch config carried 12 GiB (what the launcher printed into the container), serving_host_memory's host-read tier row carried 12 GiB, the host budget's /dev/shm field carried 12 GiB, the container's cgroup limit carried the tier's charge again, and serving_host_memory's ENGINE RECORD srv1_vllm_engine_memory — read off ~/strata-test/srv1_lil_serve.sh on 2026-10-30, when one container held the host — answered \"conversation_cache: 100 GB\" beside a 130g cap. That last copy is the one that mattered for a class this row shares with gunbc.design_argument::single_authority: gunbc.serving.serving_replica_configuration and gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile read the tier THROUGH the engine record, so the modules that reason about warmth and capacity were being shown a number no renderer had ever emitted, and any of them could have priced an obligation against it and been wrong by a factor of eight. The repair is the same move as the tier's: srv1_vllm_engine_memory now CITES srv1_lmcache_l1_configured and the new srv1_replica_container_memory_cap, its 2026-10-30 figures surviving only as provenance, and a witness pins that it cannot drift from the launch shape again. A fact with six homes does not need six checks; it needs one home. THE SIXTH HOME IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE RULE, AND THE OPERATOR'S REVIEW FOUND IT: gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_launch carried LMCACHE_L1_GB=12 in its own environment rows, and gunbc.serving.serving_provision_derivation::srv1_serving_launch_for_port COPIES those rows into every container it provisions -- a live consumer that is not the launcher, not the boot unit, and not the host budget, so the whole class of controls this row's repair landed could pass while a container came up from the model with a quarter of the tier. The tier's authority is now the figure the engine's knob actually takes, gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::srv1_lmcache_l1_configured_gib (LMCACHE_L1_GB is a whole number of gibibytes; the ByteSize is DERIVED from it, so no consumer divides bytes back into GB and rounds), and both launch paths -- provisioning environment and boot args file -- render from it, which the_tier_operand_is_the_same_number_in_the_provisioning_launch_and_the_boot_render reads out of both producers.",

    "**WHY IT LOOKED REASONABLE, WHICH IS THE PART THAT REPEATS: a cache-scaled number is defensible in isolation. 12 GiB was never measured against a working set; it was a size that left room in the container for the rest of the process. Nothing had to be wrong with it for the fleet to lose work — the policy that makes the tier load-bearing lives in the serving runtime's own cache module, outside the launcher, outside the args file, and outside every row this repository held. The size and the policy were decided by different hands at different times and joined only inside a running process, which is the boundary gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary says to stop at: the earliest point where a number is chosen without the fact that bounds it.",

    "**THE CONTRACT, STATED IN THE UNITS THAT BOUND IT: under a write-on-evict policy a checkpoint is resident while it is being staged and must not be evictable before its store completes, so the quantity that must fit in the RAM tier is the number of conversations the replica can be holding checkpoints for at once, each priced at the largest context the engine admits it to hold. That is the admitted per-replica concurrent population times the per-conversation checkpoint cost — both of which already exist as rows (the population on the replica's profile row, resolved from the host's own measured coordinate; the cost from the engine's measured KV pool density), and neither of which was ever read by the thing that decided the tier's size. Satisfying it is NECESSARY, not sufficient: it removes the silent-loss mode and promises no hit ratio, because warmth depends on the working set, the disk-tier watermark and the protection window, each with its own row.",

    "**RUNG, AS ESTABLISHED BY EXECUTED EVIDENCE AND NOT AS INTENDED: MechanicallyPreventable. The tier is now ONE row (gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::srv1_lmcache_l1_configured), the /dev/shm that carries it and the cgroup that charges it are read from that row rather than restated, and three mechanical refusals stand between a too-small tier and a running replica: the launcher render refuses (no bytes, so no digest to install), the engine-args render refuses for the same reason, and the boot admission refuses, which stops convergence from arming the replica at all — each naming which term refused (the staging floor, the arena that cannot hold the tier, the cgroup that cannot charge it) as a constructor a consumer matches rather than a string it compares. It is NOT yet StructurallyGuaranteed, and calling it that here would be the rung inflation gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder (rung honesty) names: a writable field still exists, the refusal is a separate step on its path, and the policy premise is a transcribed read rather than a produced one.",

    "**THE CONTRACT ADMITS 48 AND STILL DOES NOT KEEP ITS PROMISE, WHICH IS THE PART A READER WILL WANT TO SKIP: the floor this row demands is the in-flight population priced at the largest context the engine admits — 4 conversations x ~4.9 GiB, so ~19.6 GiB. 48 GiB clears it by 2.5x and was never going to be refused by it, and the row says so rather than pretending the number was derived to fail. What 48 does NOT clear is the rest of the promise. The tier is the only path in BOTH directions: a checkpoint being stored is resident, and a conversation returning after minutes away must LAND in the tier before the engine can read it back (the 50-75% same-card miss of 2026-11-24 is that landing failing), so the priced requirement is the population twice — ~39.3 GiB, leaving 8.7 GiB of margin, under two checkpoints. Then two terms remain that no row prices at all, and they are the reason the 06:00 UTC deletions continued: (1) the population is bounded by the WRONG EVENT. srv1 DOES admit a per-replica concurrency -- gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy emits an OwnedCap of 4 turns per replica and begin_turn reserves the turn -- so 8 sessions over a share width of 2 is not an unexamined average. What no row relates is that a checkpoint an eviction set down can still be owed its store AFTER the turn that produced it has been released: the cap bounds decode turns, not bytes in flight, and the restore half of the pair is bounded by neither. A fleet that waited 15-20 minutes for a slot is a fleet that queues, so a floor at the routed-turn count is a floor the next burst -- and every late write -- walks through; (2) no row prices the bytes that arrive WHILE a checkpoint is being written — the decode population keeps producing KV for the whole store window, and the only receipt in the tree about that window says stores were granted 48% / 43% of the bytes they asked for, so the window is longer than any bandwidth a reader would guess. Both are named as declarations (srv1_l1_missing_admitted_population, srv1_l1_missing_store_window_headroom) and carried in a typed verdict, ContractUndischarged, that the render does not suppress. A tier can therefore be admissible and undischarged at once, and only the second is the promise.",

    "**RESIDUAL, NOT COVERED BY THE REPAIR, EACH NAMED SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT HAVE TO REDISCOVER IT: (a) the store policy itself is carried with TranscribedUncited standing — it is in the serving runtime's cache module, not in the launcher or the args file, so no render control can pin what the installed image actually does; (b) --emergency-evict-for-prefetch is passed for GLM models only, so a non-GLM replica under a write-on-evict policy has no emergency-evict path at all, and this row's repair does not give it one; (c) --checkpoint-protected-share 0.5 protected nothing — outcome=none on 2172 of 2173 observations, so the knob the fleet believed was buying a protection window was not, and the tier carries the whole protection obligation alone; (d) whether the two cards could share one disk tier instead of each holding its own is open, and it bears on both the tier sizes and the host's memory headroom; (e) the two unpriced terms above, which are what separates an admitted tier from a kept promise.",

    "**THE RESIDUALS ABOVE HAVE CARRIERS, AND NAMING THEM IS WHAT MAKES THEM FOLLOW-UPS RATHER THAN ATMOSPHERE. (b) --emergency-evict-for-prefetch is a flag on the lab launcher's own face, before the -- separator, so its row belongs in extdeps.vllm.lil_serve beside the other launcher flags, TranscribedUncited under that module's own read obligation, with the decision to PASS it on a given model living in the first-party launch row gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_launch (or, if it turns out to be per-profile rather than per-launch, on the profile row in gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile). Today NO ROW ANYWHERE MENTIONS IT — the only occurrence in this repository is this receipt — so the model cannot even express the host fact that GLM replicas get an emergency valve and the Qwen replicas do not. That omission is not neutral, and it lands on this row's own contract: the emergency valve is the mechanism that makes room in the staging tier for a checkpoint ARRIVING from disk, so a replica without it has no relief on exactly the half of the contract priced by the factor of two. It is therefore a finding against the configuration, not merely an unmodelled flag. (c) --checkpoint-protected-share 0.5 has no semantic authority anywhere: extdeps.vllm.lil_serve models the SHAPE of a --env KEY=VALUE entry and its read obligation records that no LMCache source has been read, while the first-party row gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout::Srv1CheckpointRetentionPolicy describes the flag in our own voice as the policy that decides WHAT SURVIVES and for how long. THE UNJUSTIFIED LINK IS THAT FIRST-PARTY SEMANTIC CLAIM — a retention meaning read off a flag name, with no external authority behind it — and the reliance on it sits downstream of the launch row, which is precisely why 2172 of 2173 outcome=none observations could not make anything go red: no row stated a promise the observation could falsify. The frontier for this one is the extdeps read of the eviction path, not another launch row restating the share.",

    "**THE CLASS REAPPEARED INSIDE THE REPAIR ITSELF, WHICH IS WHY IT IS FILED HERE AND NOT ONLY IN A COMMIT MESSAGE. Review 98546 found both of its signatures in the first head of this row's own repair: (a) the boot gate tested ONE direction's worth of staging while the safety reading reported the pair -- the same two-answers-to-one-question drift, one level down from the tier, and with the same consequence: a configuration that cannot stage a restore boots anyway; and (b) the in-flight population was computed with truncating natural division, so a coordinate whose share width did not divide its concurrency (8 over 3) would have answered 2 where 3 conversations are in flight, shrinking a buffer's LOWER bound by a whole conversation snapshot, silently, on the unsafe side of the bound. Neither was caught by a control; both were caught by a reader. THE OPERATOR'S READ OF THE SECOND HEAD FOUND THE SAME SHAPE IN THREE MORE PLACES, each one a number answering a question it had never been asked: (c) the provisioning record carried its own copy of the tier at 12 GiB while the boot path had moved to 48, and it is the copy that reaching a real container passes through -- a second home, one consumer removed from every control that existed; (d) the conversation-snapshot price was spent on ANY launch, including one it had never been read under -- changing kv_cache_dtype alone to bf16 left admission pricing at the FP8 TP=1 figure of 20,625 bytes per token while the renderer emitted --kv-cache-dtype bf16, which is the class in its purest form: a measurement asked about a shape it never measured; and (e) the safety reading compared holds against floor-plus-headroom while REPORTING the floor, so a 40 GiB tier against a priced 47.25 GiB requirement came out as '39.25 required' with an empty missing list -- the computation done and its result discarded, which is a degradation no consumer can count. All three are controls now: a_tier_that_holds_only_one_direction_is_refused_by_the_gate_and_the_admission, the_gate_and_the_safety_reading_demand_the_same_number, the_population_rounds_up_because_it_bounds_a_buffer. THE RECOGNITION RULE EXTENDS IN TWO PLACES: a contract is only as honest as the ONE row that states its requirement -- if two consumers can hold two numbers, the guarantee is decorative; and a bound is only as honest as the arithmetic that computes it -- a division that rounds toward zero, in a quantity that bounds a buffer, is the same drift in a smaller font (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, no uncounted degradation). All five are controls now, the last three by the_price_refuses_a_launch_it_was_never_read_under, the_tier_operand_is_the_same_number_in_the_provisioning_launch_and_the_boot_render and a_priced_store_window_raises_the_gate_and_the_reading_to_the_same_number.",

    "**A PRICE AND THE SHAPE IT WAS READ UNDER ARE ONE FACT, OR THEY ARE NOTHING: the snapshot cost this contract spends came with a provenance naming an FP8 K/V pool at tensor-parallel width 1, and the row that spent it carried the bytes and the provenance but no carrier for the shape -- so the shape lived only in a sentence, and a sentence cannot refuse a launch. THE EARLIEST UNJUSTIFIED BOUNDARY OF THAT STEP IS THE PRICE ROW ITSELF: it was authored as a size, when the fact is a size-under-a-shape. It is repaired there, in a new carrier (srv1_vllm_disk_snapshot_cost_measured_under, holding the engine's dtype variant and the width the pool was read at) read by srv1_snapshot_cost_applies_to at the same site that does the multiplication, with its own refusing term (L1StagingPriceUnpriced, named apart from the too-small-tier term because 'too small' and 'cannot be priced' are different findings a consumer must tell apart). No multiplier for a shape nobody measured is in the tree, and none was guessed: a bf16 KV launch has no applicable price here, and the render says so instead of installing bytes whose margin came from arithmetic.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE, for the next host: find a tier whose size row cites a hit ratio, a working set, or a container's free space, and then find what writes it to its backing store. If the answer is 'when it leaves this tier', the size row is answering the wrong question, and the right question is a population times a cost — both of which the model already has somewhere. The tell that it is NOT this class: the backing store is written at creation, in which case the front tier really is only a cache and its size is an efficiency question.",

    "**CEILING AND ITS TELL: StructurallyGuaranteed, because a tier that is DERIVED from the population and the per-conversation cost cannot be smaller than them — there is no field left to set too low, and the policy premise arrives from the build that digests the runtime rather than from a remembered figure. The tell that the ceiling has NOT been reached: a size row still exists whose value a reader could lower, and a refusal is what stands between that edit and a running replica. Those refusals are load-bearing today, and a repair that deletes them without deriving the tier re-opens this class.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv1_lmcache_l1_configured", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout", decl_name: "srv1_l1_staging_floor", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout", decl_name: "srv1_kv_tier_verdict", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout", decl_name: "srv1_admitted_launch_config", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile", decl_name: "replica_concurrent_turns", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv1_vllm_disk_snapshot_cost", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv1_replica_container_memory_cap", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv1_vllm_engine_memory", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ]
}
